# Introduction # {#intro}

The FAIR Data Train (FDT) is a FAIR-based platform framework that aims at guaranteeing a specific
level of interoperability among its participant elements. It defines a set of application types and
their expected behaviours to support the findability, accessibility, interoperability and
reusability of data following the FAIR principles [[FAIR-PRINCIPLES]]. The FDT follows the analogy
of a train system with two main elements, *stations* and *trains*. <dfn export>Data Stations</dfn> make
data (or other kinds of digital objects) available and publish metadata about themselves and about
their content. <dfn export>Trains</dfn> represent analysis or processing algorithms that are sent to
Data Stations to process the data where it is: **the computation travels to the data, and the data
stays**. A registry indexes the metadata the stations publish, so that a client can find out which
station holds which data before sending anything anywhere.

In this document, data means not only the content of artefacts such as databases, tables and
graphs, but also other kinds of digital objects such as controlled vocabularies, ontologies and
models; unless otherwise stated the terms are used interchangeably. The FAIR Data Train
architecture defines a number of key capabilities:

* provisioning of metadata to describe digital objects;
* a common representation format [[RDF11-CONCEPTS]] to express the metadata in a machine-actionable manner;
* a common approach to inform clients on how to navigate through the metadata structure;
* a common representation format [[SHACL]] for the metadata schemas — and, since version 0.2, for what a train *requires* of a station's data and what a station's data *conforms to*, which is how a station is selected for a train;
* a common way of describing how data sources support interactions with their content, in terms of metadata properties of the station;
* a common way to represent how client applications interact with data: the interfaces and protocols by which a station accepts an incoming train, negotiates the terms under which it may run, runs it, and decides what may leave.

The main goal of the FDT general architecture is to define a set of behaviours, interfaces and
protocols to improve interoperability among data sources and data processing services. To fulfil
this goal, the FDT specifications help developers build new applications, or extend existing ones,
so that they can take part in the FAIR Data Train ecosystem. The envisioned scenario is one with a
multitude of trains, stations and client applications, independently created and able to interact
with one another because they all follow the same base guidelines — interfaces, protocols and
representation formats.

## Purpose ## {#purpose}

The purpose of this document is to present the general architecture of the FAIR Data Train: its
roles, its components, how they interact and the standards they rest on. It is primarily intended
as a reference for developers willing to add FDT functionality to their existing applications, or
to develop their own implementation of an FDT component. Knowledge of RDF, SHACL, ODRL and REST
APIs is required to follow it.

This document is the entry point to a set of specifications. Where a part of the architecture is
specified in full elsewhere, this document says what the part is for and points at the
specification that governs it. In version 0.2 that is one document: the **FDT Visit Protocol**
[[!FDT-VISIT-PROTOCOL]], which specifies the conversation between a Data Station and a Train
Handler for one visit of one train.

## How this document relates to the reference implementation ## {#reference-implementation}

*This section is non-normative.*

A reference implementation of the ecosystem exists [[FDT-COMMONS]] and this version of the
architecture was written against it: every component in [[#components]] has a running
implementation — of the two services of [[#translation-alignment]], identifier translation runs and
vocabulary alignment does not yet — the interactions of [[#interactions]] are exercised end to
end by its test suites and testbed, and the normative artefacts of the Visit Protocol — JSON
Schemas, OpenAPI documents, SHACL shapes and the fixtures every component is tested against — are
the contracts that implementation is built on. The architectural decisions behind the text are
recorded as Architecture Decision Records [[FDT-ADR]] and are cited here by number (*ADR-nnn*)
where a reader may want the reasoning rather than the result.

## Changes since version 0.1 ## {#changes}

*This section is non-normative.*

Version 0.1 (10 July 2025) described the FDT as it was first conceived. Version 0.2 brings it up
to date with what has since been built, and the following names and ideas changed:

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Version 0.1<th>Version 0.2<th>Why
  <tbody>
  <tr><td>*Personal Gateway*<td>**Individual Gateway**<td>The controller-side agent serves one controller — a company or a natural person — across every station holding their data, not only persons.
  <tr><td>*Station Directory*, "a special kind of Data Station"<td>**FDT Registry**<td>The registry is an index over what stations and depots publish and is not a trust anchor: nothing may be granted on the strength of an entry, and it is not a station.
  <tr><td>*Train Provider*<td>**Train Creator**<td>The role is the party that created a train and is answerable for it. The ontology keeps `fdt-o:TrainProvider` as the IRI with "Train Creator" as its label, because a published IRI is a promise.
  <tr><td>—<td>**Train Depot**<td>Trains are contracted assets with an identity, a creator's offer and a verifiable payload. The Depot is the authority for a train; a station resolves a train there before running it.
  <tr><td>A Data Station hosts data<td>A Data Station hosts **any digital object**<td>"Data" is meant as the FAIR principles mean it. Identifier translation between pseudonym spaces and vocabulary alignment are services a station hosts and a train visits like any other, under the same protocol and their own agreements ([[#translation-alignment]]). An earlier draft of this version made them two station types and withdrew them: they added components without adding an interface.
  <tr><td>—<td>**Visit Protocol** [[FDT-VISIT-PROTOCOL]]<td>The Content Interaction Interface of version 0.1 was a heading. It is now a specification: how a train is dispatched, how an agreement is negotiated and signed by both parties on arrival, how every decision is reported, and how a result leaves.
  <tr><td>—<td>**Conformance criteria**<td>Version 0.1 named no requirement an implementation could be checked against. [[#conformance]] now defines conforming Data Stations and Train Handlers by numbered criteria, each tied to the shape, checker or harness that decides it.
  <tr><td>Agreement signed by the station<td>Agreement signed by **both parties**<td>A record kept by one party and signed by that party alone proves nothing to a third party. Each party signs the same canonical form.
</table>

The three interface groups of the Data Station are unchanged in name; [[#datastation]] now says
what each one is.

# Roles # {#roles}

The parties of an FDT ecosystem, and what each is answerable for. A party may play several roles;
a role may be played by an organisation or, where the deployment admits it, by a natural person.

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Role<th>Party<th>Responsibility
  <tbody>
  <tr><td><dfn export>Data Controller</dfn><td>An organisation or a natural person with authority over data hosted at one or more stations<td>Authors the access conditions over their data, as ODRL offers; approves agreements where a person is required to; may revoke an agreement they granted. The identified `assigner` of every agreement over their resources. Distinct from the Station Owner (ADR-011): hosting data is not controlling it. Acts through a station's console or, across stations, through an Individual Gateway.
  <tr><td><dfn export>Station Owner</dfn><td>The organisation (or, in the personal profile, the person) operating a station<td>Sets station-wide policy — quotas, permitted mechanisms, networks served — orthogonal to the controllers' conditions. The effective decision over a request is the conjunction of the controller's offer and the owner's policy.
  <tr><td><dfn export>Data Consumer</dfn> / <dfn export>Train Owner</dfn><td>The party accountable for a run<td>The `assignee` of every agreement a run concludes; submits trains through a Train Handler and receives inspected, non-personal results.
  <tr><td><dfn export>Train Creator</dfn><td>The party that created a train and publishes it<td>Offers the train as a contracted asset through a Train Depot, under their own licence; signs what they publish. When a creator sends their own train, they also play the Train Owner role.
  <tr><td>Train Handler operator<td>Runs dispatch and orchestration infrastructure<td>Not a party to any agreement; may hold aggregate results only (ADR-015).
  <tr><td>Registry operator, Train Depot operator<td>Run a registry or a depot<td>A registry harvests and indexes; it is never a trust anchor. A depot is the authority for the trains it holds — bytes, digests, declared parameters and output, the creator's offer and keys.
  <tr><td>Trusted third party<td>Operates a station hosting an identifier-translation service<td>A Station Owner whose station translates pseudonyms between organisational spaces — the only party that ever holds both sides of a join. An alignment service has an operator in the same sense, with nothing sensitive in their keeping.
  <tr><td><dfn export>Network governance authority</dfn><td>One per FDT network<td>Participation rules, evidence categories, trusted identity issuers and trust anchors for the network (ADR-017).
</table>

# Components # {#components}

<figure id="fig-ecosystem">
  <img src="images/fdt-ecosystem-components.svg" width="1641" height="785" alt="The components of an FDT ecosystem in four groups. Consumer side: the Train Handler and the Train Creator's publications. Controller side: the Individual Gateway. Federation services: the FDT Registry, the Train Depot and the governance function. Data Stations, which may belong to several networks and host any digital object — datasets, identifier-translation and alignment services alike. Underneath all of them, fdt-commons: the shared contracts every component validates against. Solid lines are version 1 interactions; dotted lines are version 2 or governance.">
  <figcaption>The components of an FDT ecosystem, and the shared contracts under them</figcaption>
</figure>

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Component<th>Responsibility
  <tbody>
  <tr><td><dfn export>Data Station</dfn><td>Hosts digital objects of any kind — datasets, and services such as identifier translation or vocabulary alignment; publishes FAIR metadata about itself and what it hosts; receives trains; negotiates an agreement per visit on arrival; enforces policy at three checkpoints; executes the train through an interaction-mechanism adapter; returns the inspected result, or lets inspected state travel onward. [[#datastation]].
  <tr><td><dfn export>Train Handler</dfn><td>The consumer-side agent: selects and parametrises a train, composes a plan, dispatches visits to stations (push or poll), orchestrates multi-station itineraries, collects the non-personal results and provenance, and shows the run as it happens. The Handler owns the run; the station owns the visit. [[#trainhandler]].
  <tr><td><dfn export>Individual Gateway</dfn><td>The controller-side agent for one controller across every station hosting their data: author and revoke access conditions, review the agreements concluded over their data and the audit slice that touched it, receive and decide approval requests, exercise opt-out. [[#gateway]].
  <tr><td><dfn export>FDT Registry</dfn><td>A metadata registry: harvests and indexes what stations and depots publish, and answers which stations hold the data a train needs. An index and never a trust anchor. [[#registry]].
  <tr><td><dfn export>Train Depot</dfn><td>The authority for a train and a catalogue of train offers: payload bytes and digest, declared parameters and output, the creator's licence as an ODRL offer and the creator's signing keys. [[#depot]].
  <tr><td>Governance function<td>Per network: onboarding, evidence, trusted issuers and trust anchors, rule publication; exposes machine-readable membership so stations and handlers can check who belongs to which network. [[#networks]].
  <tr><td><dfn export>fdt-commons</dfn><td>The shared contracts: the FDT ontology and profiles, SHACL shapes, JSON-LD contexts, JSON Schemas and OpenAPI documents, state machines and their mapping to the Dataspace Protocol, and the fixtures every component is tested against. [[#contracts]].
</table>

Trains are packaged, executable interactions of four families — query, API, script and
container — and are **assets, not datasets**: a train is something a station runs, not something
it hosts (ADR-020). A train has an identity and an offer of its own, carries a payload with a
digest, and declares the data it needs (as a SHACL model) and the output it produces (as a JSON
Schema).

## Data Station ## {#datastation}

Since the purpose of the FAIR Data Train architecture is to define a set of desired behaviours that
applications expose and support, the definition of the Data Station is the definition of its
interface. The interface is divided into three groups, as depicted in <a href="#fig-fds">the figure below</a>: the
<dfn export>Metadata Interface</dfn>, the <dfn export>Station Interface</dfn> and the <dfn export>Content Interaction
Interface</dfn>. Each group exposes the interfaces of a number of services the station provides,
and the interaction with the data happens only through the station's interaction component, which
is the one component that connects to the data storage.

A station hosts **digital objects of any kind** — "data" in the sense the FAIR principles give the
word. Hosted datasets and their parts are the common case; a service that translates identifiers
between pseudonym spaces or aligns vocabularies is hosted the same way, described in the same
catalogue with the interaction mechanism it is reached by, and visited by a train under the same
protocol and its own agreement ([[#translation-alignment]]).

<figure id="fig-fds">
  <img src="images/fds_simplified_view.png" alt="The FDS API exposes a Metadata Interface, a Station Interface and a Content Interaction Interface, each realised by a service and a component; only the Interaction Component connects to the Data Storage." width="80%">
  <figcaption>Interface groups of the Data Station</figcaption>
</figure>

### Metadata Interface ### {#metadatainterface}

The Metadata Interface publishes what the station holds and on whose terms, as RDF, following the
FAIR Data Point specification [[FDP]] for the metadata structure and its navigation. A station
publishes:

* a **catalogue** [[VOCAB-DCAT-3]] of the hosted datasets it serves and of the dataset *parts* a controller offers in their own right, each with its distributions per interaction mechanism, its `dcat:theme`, and the ODRL offer [[ODRL-MODEL]] that states the controller's access conditions over it;
* for each dataset or part, what its data **conforms to**: a SHACL shape [[SHACL]], served by the station alongside the catalogue. This is what makes station selection answerable — a train declares the data it needs as a SHACL model, and a station matches when the shapes it publishes cover that model (ADR-030). Where the station's data is not natively RDF, the shape is generated from the mapping the dataset declares [[RML]] and the mapping is published too.

Publishing a catalogue entry grants nothing. A catalogue says what exists and under which terms;
every access is still negotiated on arrival against the offer, per visit.

### Station Interface ### {#stationinterface}

The Station Interface publishes the station's **self-description**: its type and the interaction
mechanisms it supports, the networks it is a member of and the governance authority of each, the
dispatch modes it accepts (push, poll, or both), the protocol versions it implements, and links to
its metadata and catalogue. The self-description is what a Train Handler reads to know whether a
station will accept a train from a given network and how to reach it: the station's dispatch
endpoint is a `dcat:DataService` whose `dct:conformsTo` names the Visit Protocol version.

### Content Interaction Interface ### {#contentinteractioninterface}

The Content Interaction Interface is the **Visit Protocol** [[!FDT-VISIT-PROTOCOL]]: the
conversation between a Train Handler and a Data Station for one visit — one train, at one
station, under one agreement. It covers dispatch, negotiation on arrival, the signatures of both
parties, every checkpoint decision as an event, execution progress, result delivery and the
hand-over of onward state. It is deliberately the only interface a station has to implement to
take part in any itinerary pattern. A Data Station conforms to this architecture by conforming to
that protocol as a station.

Behind the interface a station enforces policy at three checkpoints, with negotiation between the
first two:

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Checkpoint<th>Decides
  <tbody>
  <tr><td><dfn export>PEP 1</dfn> — arrival<td>Is the caller who they say, is the descriptor valid, is the payload the one the train's depot offer names (by digest), does this station support the mechanism. A failure here **rejects** the visit.
  <tr><td><dfn export>NEG</dfn> — negotiation<td>Is the request satisfiable under the controller's offer for the visit's network and the owner's policy. Produces an agreement, or asks a person where a machine may not decide, or **refuses** — a controller's decision, never a checkpoint's.
  <tr><td><dfn export>PEP 2</dfn> — before execution<td>Is the agreement active and signed by both parties, is it about this visit, do the station's quotas allow the run.
  <tr><td><dfn export>PEP 3</dfn> — after execution<td>Does the result conform to the train's declared output, does every duty of the agreement hold on it, what class of state may leave and where to. A failure here **rejects** the result; nothing leaves.
</table>

## Train Handler ## {#trainhandler}

The Train Handler is the consumer's agent. It resolves a train from a Train Depot, asks the
registry which stations hold the data the train needs, composes a <dfn export>plan</dfn> — the train, its
parameter values, an itinerary and the consumer's purpose as an ODRL request template — and runs
it: one <dfn export>run</dfn> is one execution of a plan, decomposed into <dfn export>visits</dfn>, each one
train execution at one station under one agreement. The Handler dispatches each visit by push or,
for a station that accepts no inbound connection, by letting the station poll for it; follows the
station's event stream; signs the assignee's side of each agreement with the train owner's key;
applies the plan's failure policy to each outcome; and assembles the run record and the
**completeness statement** — for every intended station, its outcome in the protocol's own words,
its cause and the station's stated reason.

The Handler holds **non-personal, aggregate results only** (ADR-015). Record-level data never
leaves the station that hosts it, except to another station under an agreement.

## Individual Gateway ## {#gateway}

The Individual Gateway is one controller's agent across every station hosting data they control.
Through it a controller authors access conditions once and publishes them to each station, reviews
the agreements concluded over their data and the slice of each station's audit trail that touched
it, receives approval requests where a person must decide and decides them, and exercises
revocation and opt-out. A controller may also act directly at a station's own console; the Gateway
is what makes the same controller's view coherent across stations.

## FDT Registry ## {#registry}

The registry harvests the self-descriptions and catalogues that stations and depots publish and
indexes them: stations by interaction mechanism, network, theme, the shapes their data conforms to
and the join keys they offer; trains by what they require. Its central question is **which stations
hold the data this train needs**, answered by structural coverage of the train's data requirement
by a station's published shapes — and, for a near miss, by naming the property a station lacks.
A registry is an index: nothing may be granted on the strength of an entry, a station may be
indexed by several registries, and a Handler acts on what a station publishes, not on what a
registry harvested.

## Train Depot ## {#depot}

The Train Depot is the authority for a train. It holds the payload bytes and their digest, the
train's declared parameters, data requirement and output, the creator's licence over the train as
an ODRL offer, and the creator's public signing keys; it publishes them as a catalogue of train
offers. A station resolves a train at the Depot before anything runs and verifies that the payload
it was handed is the one the Depot describes. Taking a train from a Depot is itself a two-party
agreement — the creator's licence, signed by the creator's Gateway and by the would-be owner — and
a Depot signs nothing in anybody's name.

## Identifier translation and vocabulary alignment ## {#translation-alignment}

A multi-station analysis across organisational boundaries needs two services that no single
data-holding station provides: translating identifiers between pseudonym spaces, and aligning
vocabularies. Both are **hosted by Data Stations like any other digital object** and reached by
the same protocol under their own agreements; neither is a component of the architecture.

**Identifier translation.** A station whose interaction mechanism is identifier translation
(`fdt-inst:IdentifierTranslation`) receives a list of pseudonyms in one organisation's space and
returns the corresponding list in another's — **one side only, never the pair**, and in an order
that discloses no correspondence — so that two stations can be joined at the level of the
individual by nobody but the trusted third party that operates it. The station declares the
pseudonym spaces and join keys it serves in its catalogue, and an existing trusted third party is
wrapped as such a station.

**Vocabulary alignment.** A station hosting mappings between code systems, units, spatial and
administrative divisions, which a train consults or visits as a hop; stations declare per dataset
the vocabularies they use, so a Handler can check at planning time that two stations' data can be
put together.

The FDT ontology carries `fdt-o:LinkageStation` and `fdt-o:AlignmentStation` as subclasses of
`fdt-o:DataStation`: labels a station may add to its self-description so that a registry finds it
by what it hosts. They introduce no interface and no requirement that the mechanism declaration
does not already carry.

## Networks and governance ## {#networks}

An <dfn export>FDT network</dfn> is a set of participants that accept one governance authority's
rules and trust anchors. A station frequently serves several communities at once, so **a station
may belong to several networks with different governance** (ADR-017). The station's
self-description lists its memberships; policy is evaluated per network, because a controller's
offer may differ per network; every agreement records the network it was concluded under; and
identity is issued by network authorities, not by the FDT — in version 1 through federated OpenID
Connect providers the network trusts [[OPENID-CONNECT-CORE]].

A network also states whether a station in it **may conclude a negotiation without a person**.
Where it may not, the station still evaluates the request and produces a recommendation with its
evidence, and a person decides; where a machine may not grant, it may not refuse either (ADR-032).

## Shared contracts ## {#contracts}

Interoperability rests on shared contracts, not on shared code. `fdt-commons` [[FDT-COMMONS]]
holds the FDT ontology [[FDT-O]] and the ODRL and run-description profiles; the SHACL shapes that
every published document and every arriving request is validated against; the JSON Schemas and
OpenAPI documents of the Visit Protocol and of the other component interfaces; the state machines
and their mapping to the Dataspace Protocol [[DSP]]; and the fixtures every implementation is
tested against, including counter-examples that must fail. No component redefines a term, shape,
schema or endpoint that exists there, and a change to a contract is made there first.

# Interactions # {#interactions}

## Plan, run, visit ## {#plan-run-visit}

A plan names a train, its parameter values, an itinerary — the pattern, the entry stations or a
registry query that selects them, phases and dependencies, stop conditions, budget — and the
consumer's purpose as an ODRL request template. A run is one execution of a plan. A visit is one
train execution at one station under one agreement. Every itinerary pattern — a single visit,
fan-out over many stations, a sequence, a discovery chain that learns where to go next from what it
finds, iterative rounds, two-phase selection, composition — decomposes into visits, which is why
the Visit Protocol is the only interface a station needs.

## Agreements, per visit, signed by both parties ## {#agreements}

Every visit — including a visit for identifier translation or vocabulary alignment — is governed by its own ODRL
agreement, negotiated on arrival (ADR-014). The station matches the train's request against the
controller's offer for the visit's network and the owner's policy and **derives** the agreement:
it grants exactly the actions requested that the offer permits, carries every usage constraint
bounded by what was offered, carries the offer's prohibitions and duties verbatim, records the
eligibility facts it established as evidence rather than as live terms, and pins the offer, the
request and the train's payload by digest. There is no counter-offer: narrowing is always
permitted and widening never is.

The agreement is then **signed by both parties over the same canonical form**: the station signs
the assigner's side — with the controller's key where they hold one, otherwise as the controller's
agent, and the signature says which — and the Train Handler signs the assignee's side with the
train owner's key. An agreement only one party has signed is not an agreement, and nothing runs
under one. Both parties keep a pinned copy; neither can rewrite history unnoticed by the other, and
a third party holding the document can verify it without asking either.

## Dispatch, checkpoints and outcomes ## {#dispatch}

<figure id="fig-visit">
  <img src="visit-protocol/diagrams/seq-push-dispatch.svg" width="960" height="820" alt="Sequence diagram of one visit under push dispatch: descriptor pushed, PEP 1, negotiation, the station's signature, the Handler's signature, PEP 2, execution, PEP 3, delivery; every decision an event.">
  <figcaption>One visit, push-dispatched: the Handler pushes a descriptor, the station decides at each checkpoint and reports every decision as an event, both parties sign the agreement, and the inspected result is delivered</figcaption>
</figure>

A visit is dispatched by **push** — the Handler posts a visit descriptor to the station — or by
**poll** — a station that accepts no inbound connection fetches the descriptors waiting for it
from the Handler. Beyond the gateway the two are processed identically (ADR-004). The station then
runs the visit through its checkpoints ([[#contentinteractioninterface]]), and **every decision
it takes is an event with a justification** (ADR-027): the ordered event stream is the source of
truth for what happened to a visit, for the Handler's view of the run and for the audit trail a
controller reads.

Every visit ends in exactly one of seven outcomes, and the words are part of the contract (ADR-026),
because a completeness statement reports them to a data consumer verbatim:

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Outcome<th>Meaning<th>Whose
  <tbody>
  <tr><td><dfn export>Delivered</dfn><td>The result passed inspection and left the station (possibly redacted to meet a threshold).<td>—
  <tr><td><dfn export>Refused</dfn><td>Access was declined at negotiation — by the controller's standing conditions or by a person.<td>A **controller's** decision, with their stated reason
  <tr><td><dfn export>Rejected</dfn><td>A checkpoint's verdict on a document or a result: an invalid descriptor, a payload that is not the one offered, a result a duty forbids.<td>A **checkpoint's** verdict; nobody decided anything
  <tr><td><dfn export>TimedOut</dfn><td>A person was asked and the wait ran out, or the Handler withdrew the visit.<td>**Nobody** decided
  <tr><td><dfn export>Failed</dfn><td>Machinery broke.<td>—
  <tr><td><dfn export>Revoked</dfn><td>The controller took the agreement out of force while the visit was queued or running.<td>A **controller's** act over an agreement they granted
  <tr><td><dfn export>Skipped</dfn><td>The run never asked this station — a phase condition excluded it, a chain was pruned, the failure policy dropped it.<td>The **Handler's** record; no station can report it about itself
</table>

These are not shades of one fact. Reporting a controller's refusal as a rejection tells a consumer
their request failed a check when a person declined it; reporting a timeout as either puts a
verdict in the mouth of somebody who never gave one. Implementations keep the vocabulary exact in
code, logs and user interfaces.

## State between hops ## {#state-between-hops}

Three mechanisms carry state from one visit to the next, chosen per plan and constrained by the
stations' policies (ADR-016): **inside the train** — a container or script train carries state, and
the origin station's PEP 3 judges what may leave with it; **via the Handler** — only non-personal
state returns to the Handler and is re-injected as parameters; **via an identifier-translation hop** — only
opaque pseudonyms cross an organisational boundary, translated at a station a trusted third party
operates and handed to the next station **by location and digest, never by value**, so that the Handler holds a
name and the bytes stay at the station that produced them.

# Standards # {#standards}

*This section is non-normative.*

The FDT is built on open standards, and this table says how each is used — *used* where the
reference implementation reads or writes it as specified, *aligned* where the FDT is designed to
map onto it, *declared* where the architecture names it and the implementation does not yet
exercise it.

<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Standard<th>Where<th>Status
  <tbody>
  <tr><td>RDF 1.1 [[RDF11-CONCEPTS]], JSON-LD 1.1 [[JSON-LD11]]<td>All metadata, catalogues, self-descriptions, offers, requests and agreements; the Visit Protocol's RDF payloads.<td>used
  <tr><td>DCAT 3 [[VOCAB-DCAT-3]], FAIR Data Point [[FDP]]<td>Station catalogues (datasets, parts, distributions, services); the metadata structure and its navigation.<td>used
  <tr><td>SHACL [[SHACL]]<td>Validation of every published document and arriving request; a train's data requirement and a station's data description, matched by coverage.<td>used
  <tr><td>ODRL 2.2 [[ODRL-MODEL]] [[ODRL-VOCAB]] with the FDT profile<td>Access conditions (offers), requests, agreements and the derivation rule between them; the purpose vocabulary from DPV [[DPV]].<td>used
  <tr><td>PROV-O [[PROV-O]]<td>The run record: a run is an activity, its visits sub-activities, the merged result a derivation from the delivered visits.<td>used
  <tr><td>JSON Schema [[JSON-SCHEMA]], OpenAPI 3.1 [[OPENAPI]]<td>The Visit Protocol's three message types and every HTTP interface in the ecosystem.<td>used
  <tr><td>OpenID Connect [[OPENID-CONNECT-CORE]], OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange [[RFC8693]]<td>Federated identity, trusted per network; the acting person carried as RFC 8693's actor claim; a graded dispatch credential.<td>used
  <tr><td>Ed25519 [[RFC8032]], JSON Web Key [[RFC7517]], RDF Dataset Canonicalization [[RDF-CANON]]<td>Signatures of both parties over the canonical form of an agreement and of a train's submission; keys published as JWK sets.<td>used
  <tr><td>RFC 9457 Problem Details [[RFC9457]]<td>Every error a component returns, carrying the FDT outcome word and the checkpoint.<td>used
  <tr><td>Dataspace Protocol 2025-1 [[DSP]]<td>Agreement and visit lifecycles are mapped onto Contract Negotiation and Transfer Process states; the mapping is documentation in version 1 and a binding in version 2 (ADR-019).<td>aligned
  <tr><td>RML [[RML]]<td>Where a station's data is not RDF, the mapping its published shape is generated from.<td>declared
  <tr><td>Verifiable Credentials<td>The identity model is kept credential-ready so that W3C Verifiable Credentials can replace bearer tokens without a schema migration (ADR-010); eligibility evidence is where they would first apply (ADR-031).<td>declared
</table>

# Conformance # {#conformance}

## Conformance classes ## {#conformance-classes}

This document defines conformance for the two components every itinerary needs, and states what
each must do beyond the Visit Protocol. The key words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD and MAY are those of
[[!RFC2119]]. The protocol-level requirements — dispatch, negotiation, signatures, checkpoints,
events, envelopes, hand-over — are the Visit Protocol's and are collected as numbered criteria
there [[!FDT-VISIT-PROTOCOL]]; the criteria below are the architecture's own and are numbered
apart so that a conformance claim can cite both without ambiguity.

: <dfn export>Conforming Data Station</dfn>
:: A component that conforms to the Visit Protocol as a push-dispatch station or as a
    poll-dispatch station, and meets every criterion of [[#criteria-ds]] — the last four of them
    where it hosts an identifier-translation or alignment service.
: <dfn export>Conforming Train Handler</dfn>
:: A component that conforms to the Visit Protocol as a Train Handler, and meets every criterion
    of [[#criteria-th]].

Conformance for the Individual Gateway, the FDT Registry and the Train Depot will be specified in their own documents as those are published; their interfaces exist
today only as OpenAPI documents and fixtures in `fdt-commons` [[FDT-COMMONS]], and a claim about
them is a claim against those artefacts.

## How the criteria are checked ## {#criteria-checking}

The criteria below are checked in the three ways the Visit Protocol defines: by **schema** — a
SHACL shape or JSON Schema in `fdt-commons` decides it, and the fixtures named are what an
implementation must accept and the counter-examples it must refuse, each with the message the
shape carries; by **rule** — a checker in `fdt-commons/tools` executes it over the fixtures
named; or by **harness** — a behaviour observable only against a live counterpart, for which no
third-party-runnable harness is yet published and the reference implementation's test suites are
the only published evidence. The artefacts are those pinned by the Visit Protocol's Appendix B.

## Criteria for a Data Station ## {#criteria-ds}

<table class="data criteria">
  <thead><tr><th>Id<th>Requirement<th>Stated in<th>Checked by
  <tbody>
  <tr id="ds-1"><td>DS-1<td>A Data Station MUST conform to the Visit Protocol as a push-dispatch station, as a poll-dispatch station, or as both.<td>[[#contentinteractioninterface]]<td>The Visit Protocol's criteria.
  <tr id="ds-2"><td>DS-2<td>A Data Station MUST publish a self-description typed `fdt-o:DataStation` stating its type, the interaction mechanisms it supports, the networks it is a member of with the governance authority of each, the dispatch modes it accepts, the protocol versions it implements and links to its metadata and catalogue; its dispatch endpoint MUST be a `dcat:DataService` whose `dct:conformsTo` names the Visit Protocol version.<td>[[#stationinterface]]<td>Schema — `fdts:StationSelfDescriptionShape`, `fdts:MembershipShape`, `fdts:DataServiceShape` (`station-catalog.shapes.ttl`) over the stations of `network-and-stations.ttl`. Harness for the live document.
  <tr id="ds-3"><td>DS-3<td>A Data Station MUST publish a catalogue typed `fdt-o:StationCatalog`, published by itself, listing the hosted datasets (`fdt-o:HostedDataset`) it serves and the parts (`fdt-o:DatasetPart`) a controller offers in their own right; every dataset and part MUST carry at least one distribution per interaction mechanism it is reachable by, an ODRL offer by its controller, and a `dcat:theme` that is a concept IRI.<td>[[#metadatainterface]]<td>Schema — `fdts:StationCatalogShape`, `fdts:DatasetShape`, `fdts:DatasetPartShape`, `fdts:GovernedDataThemeShape`, `fdts:MechanismDistributionShape`, refusing `dataset-without-offer.ttl`, `dataset-part-without-offer.ttl`, `theme-written-as-a-keyword.ttl`, `offer-whose-target-no-graph-describes.ttl` (`published-graph.shapes.ttl`).
  <tr id="ds-4"><td>DS-4<td>For every dataset and part a Data Station MUST publish what its data conforms to — at least one SHACL node shape named by `dct:conformsTo` — and MUST serve those shapes beside the catalogue, so that a train's data requirement can be matched against them by structural coverage.<td>[[#metadatainterface]], [[#registry]]<td>Schema — `fdts:DatasetShape` refusing `dataset-without-a-shape.ttl`. Rule — `coverage.py check` against `tests/coverage-expectations.json`, which records the misses as well as the hits.
  <tr id="ds-5"><td>DS-5<td>A Data Station MUST follow the FAIR Data Point specification for the structure and navigation of its metadata.<td>[[#metadatainterface]]<td>Harness.
  <tr id="ds-6"><td>DS-6<td>Publishing a catalogue entry MUST grant nothing: a Data Station MUST negotiate every access on arrival against the controller's offer, per visit.<td>[[#metadatainterface]], [[#agreements]]<td>Harness (the Visit Protocol's negotiation criteria).
  <tr id="ds-7"><td>DS-7<td>A Data Station MUST evaluate the controller's offers for the network a visit runs under and for no other, MUST record that network in every agreement, and MUST verify a dispatching party's credential against the identity providers that network trusts.<td>[[#networks]]<td>Schema — `fdts:PolicyCommonShape` (`fdt-p:underNetwork` required of every offer, request and agreement). Harness.
  <tr id="ds-8"><td>DS-8<td>In a network that states a station in it may not conclude a negotiation without a person, a Data Station MUST neither grant nor refuse by machine: it MUST evaluate, produce a recommendation with its evidence, and put the case to a person.<td>[[#networks]]<td>Schema — `fdts:NetworkShape` refusing `network-that-does-not-say-who-may-decide.ttl`; the decision shapes of `policy.shapes.ttl` refusing `automatic-agreement-naming-a-decider.ttl`, `human-decision-with-nothing-to-review.ttl`. Harness.
  <tr id="ds-9"><td>DS-9<td>A Data Station MUST resolve a train at its Depot before anything runs and verify that the payload it was handed is the one the Depot describes; it MUST treat a train as an executable asset and MUST NOT treat it as a `dcat:Dataset`.<td>[[#depot]], [[#components]]<td>Schema — `fdts:TrainOfferShape` refusing `train-typed-as-dataset.ttl`. Rule — `payloads.py check`.
  <tr id="ds-10"><td>DS-10<td>A Data Station MUST enforce policy at the three checkpoints and MUST emit every decision it takes as an event with a justification; it MUST NOT change the state of a visit silently.<td>[[#contentinteractioninterface]], [[#dispatch]]<td>The Visit Protocol's criteria.
  <tr id="ds-11"><td>DS-11<td>Record-level data MUST NOT leave a Data Station except to another station, under an agreement, by location and digest; it MUST NOT appear in a result, an artefact, an event or a justification.<td>[[#trainhandler]], [[#state-between-hops]]<td>Schema — `fdts:VisitShape` refusing `visit-record-level-onward.ttl`. Rule — `inspection.py check` refusing `record-level-onward.json`.
  <tr id="ds-12"><td>DS-12<td>A Data Station MUST report every visit's outcome in one of the seven words of [[#dispatch]], exactly, in its events, envelopes, logs and user interfaces.<td>[[#dispatch]]<td>The Visit Protocol's criteria.
  <tr id="ds-13"><td>DS-13<td>A Data Station that hosts an identifier-translation service MUST declare it in its self-description as a `dcat:DataService` of the mechanism `fdt-inst:IdentifierTranslation`, and MUST publish a catalogue like any other station.<td>[[#translation-alignment]]<td>Schema — `fdts:LinkageStationServiceShape` (`station-catalog.shapes.ttl`), which selects a station by the label `fdt-o:LinkageStation` the ontology keeps for this capability, refusing `linkage-station-that-publishes-no-catalogue.ttl`, `linkage-station-that-publishes-no-translation-service.ttl`.
  <tr id="ds-14"><td>DS-14<td>A translation or alignment service MUST be visited as a hop under its own agreement, through the Visit Protocol.<td>[[#translation-alignment]], [[#agreements]]<td>Schema — `visit-result-ttg-hop2.json` (the translation visit), `visit-descriptor-ttg-hop3.json` (the hop that receives its hand-over). Harness.
  <tr id="ds-15"><td>DS-15<td>A translation MUST return the identifiers of one pseudonym space only and never the pair, in an order that discloses no correspondence with the order asked, and the count it reports MUST be the count it released.<td>[[#translation-alignment]]<td>Schema — `translation-envelope.schema.json` over `translation-result-ttg-hop2.json`. Rule — `inspection.py check` refusing `translation-that-returns-the-space-it-was-given.json`, `translation-in-the-order-it-was-asked.json`, `translation-whose-count-is-not-what-it-released.json`.
  <tr id="ds-16"><td>DS-16<td>A Data Station MUST hand translated state to the next station by location and digest and never by value.<td>[[#state-between-hops]]<td>The Visit Protocol's criteria (`onward.handedOverDigest`, the inbound rules).
</table>

## Criteria for a Train Handler ## {#criteria-th}

<table class="data criteria">
  <thead><tr><th>Id<th>Requirement<th>Stated in<th>Checked by
  <tbody>
  <tr id="th-1"><td>TH-1<td>A Train Handler MUST conform to the Visit Protocol as a Train Handler.<td>[[#trainhandler]]<td>The Visit Protocol's criteria.
  <tr id="th-2"><td>TH-2<td>Every plan a Train Handler runs MUST conform to the run-description shapes: a train, its parameter values, an itinerary with its pattern, a failure policy, an approval wait in days or less, and the consumer's purpose as a request template; an open-ended pattern MUST carry a budget and a stop condition; a two-phase plan MUST carry the condition that selects the second phase's stations.<td>[[#plan-run-visit]]<td>Schema — `fdts:PlanShape`, `fdts:ItineraryShape`, `fdts:OpenEndedItineraryShape`, `fdts:TwoPhaseItineraryShape`, `fdts:FailurePolicyShape` (`run.shapes.ttl`) over the plans of `trains-and-plans.ttl` and the `plan-*.jsonld` fixtures, refusing `plan-discovery-without-budget.ttl`, `two-phase-whose-second-phase-selects-nobody.ttl`, `plan-approval-wait-of-one-month.ttl`, `plan-approval-wait-typed-xsd-duration.ttl`.
  <tr id="th-3"><td>TH-3<td>A station selector MUST state the data a station must hold, as a SHACL model, and a Train Handler MUST select stations by structural coverage of that requirement by the shapes each station publishes — acting on what a station publishes, never on a registry's entry alone.<td>[[#registry]], [[#plan-run-visit]]<td>Schema — `fdts:StationSelectorShape` refusing `selector-without-data-requirement.ttl`. Rule — `coverage.py check` against `tests/coverage-expectations.json`. Harness.
  <tr id="th-4"><td>TH-4<td>A Train Handler MUST dispatch query and API trains orchestrated only.<td>[[#state-between-hops]]<td>Schema — `fdts:QueryTrainCoordinationShape` refusing `plan-query-train-choreographed.ttl`.
  <tr id="th-5"><td>TH-5<td>A Train Handler MUST resolve a train at its Depot and dispatch only a train its owner holds under the creator's licence, concluded as a two-party agreement signed by the creator and by the owner; it MUST NOT dispatch a withdrawn train.<td>[[#depot]]<td>Schema — `train-offer.shapes.ttl` (`fdts:TrainOfferShape` and the withdrawal shape of ADR-036) over `train-taken.ttl`, refusing `train-taken-from-someone-who-did-not-write-it.ttl`, `train-offer-assigned-by-its-depot.ttl`, `train-withdrawn-by-nobody.ttl`; Rule — `derivation.py` refusing `depot-signed-the-creators-side.ttl`. The Depot interface itself is specified only by `train-depot-api.yaml` and `protocol/train-publication.md` until its document is published.
  <tr id="th-6"><td>TH-6<td>A Train Handler MUST hold non-personal, aggregate results only — envelopes, events, aggregates and the names of hand-overs — and MUST NOT store record-level data.<td>[[#trainhandler]]<td>The Visit Protocol's criteria. Harness.
  <tr id="th-7"><td>TH-7<td>A Train Handler MUST produce a completeness statement for every run that did not deliver from every intended station, naming every intended station and, for each, its outcome in the seven words verbatim, its cause and the station's stated reason.<td>[[#trainhandler]], [[#dispatch]]<td>Schema — `fdts:RunShape`, `fdts:CompletenessShape` refusing `run-partial-without-completeness.ttl`; `run-state.schema.json` over `run-state-two-phase.json`. Harness.
  <tr id="th-8"><td>TH-8<td>A Train Handler MUST record each run as PROV-O provenance: the run an activity, its visits sub-activities, the merged result a derivation from the delivered visits.<td>[[#trainhandler]], [[#standards]]<td>Harness. No shape in `fdt-commons` 0.75.0 constrains the run record beyond `fdts:RunShape`; this is a recorded gap of the conformance material.
  <tr id="th-9"><td>TH-9<td>A Train Handler MUST visit under its own credential, issued by a provider the visit's network trusts, and MUST NOT forward a consumer's credential to a station.<td>[[#networks]]<td>The Visit Protocol's criteria.
</table>

## Conformance statement ## {#conformance-statement}

An implementation claims conformance to this architecture by stating the class claimed, the
Visit Protocol conformance statement it rests on, the `fdt-commons` version and commit it was
validated against, and, for each criterion above, that it accepts the fixtures named and refuses
the counter-examples named with their recorded messages, or the evidence it offers where only a
harness can decide. The reference implementation is the only implementation that can make a
third-party-verified claim today, and this document says so rather than imply otherwise.
